-- Passage rg7-authors-purpose-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5b5d075c-0f95-5b9b-8c2d-a0a53139d12c', 'rg7-authors-purpose-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$A Bat Is Not Blind$q$, $q$Many people use the phrase "blind as a bat" to describe someone who cannot see well. The saying is popular, but it gives bats far too little credit. Every kind of bat has eyes, and none of them is blind.

The confusion probably comes from echolocation. Many bats that hunt insects at night make high sounds and listen for the echoes. The returning sound tells them where a moth is and how fast it is moving. Because this skill is so impressive, some people assume bats must use it because their eyes do not work.

That assumption is mistaken. Bats rely on more than one sense, just as people do. Fruit bats, which are some of the largest bats, have big eyes and see well in dim light. Most of them do not use echolocation at all. They find ripe fruit by sight and smell. Even insect-eating bats use their eyes to notice the shapes of trees and hills when they fly long distances.

Scientists have also found that some bats can see kinds of light that people cannot, such as certain ultraviolet light. This ability may help them find flowers that reflect that light.

The next time you hear someone say "blind as a bat," you might offer a better comparison. A bat is not a creature missing a sense. It is a creature with an extra one.$q$, 'draft')
