-- Passage rg7-authors-purpose-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('bc9354b3-3a1b-5c23-a5c7-7e0897a592a9', 'rg7-authors-purpose-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Game Time for Every Player$q$, $q$At Ridgeview Middle School, the basketball teams each have fifteen players. In most games, only seven or eight of them ever leave the bench. Our coaches say they play the strongest players so the team has the best chance to win. I respect our coaches, but I believe every player who comes to practice should get at least a few minutes in each game.

Middle school sports are supposed to help students learn and grow. A player cannot get better at handling pressure if she never faces any. Practice helps, but it is not the same as a real game with a crowd and a clock. Students who sit all season often quit before high school, and some of them might have become strong players with more experience.

Supporters of the current system say that playing everyone would cause the team to lose more games. They also say that earning a spot teaches players to work hard. These points matter. Nobody enjoys losing, and hard work should be rewarded. However, a rule of four minutes per player would still leave most of each game for the top players. Players would still compete for starting spots and longer minutes.

Several schools in our league already follow a rule like this. Their win and loss records are about the same as ours, and they keep more players on their teams from year to year.

Winning a middle school game feels great for an evening. Learning to love a sport can last a lifetime. Our athletic department should adopt a minimum playing time rule.$q$, 'draft')
