-- Passage rg7-authors-purpose-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('71a7b235-a699-57a7-b069-49186598661c', 'rg7-authors-purpose-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Leave Some Leaves$q$, $q$Every autumn, the sound of rakes and leaf blowers fills our neighborhood. Many homeowners believe a neat yard is a healthy yard, so they bag every leaf and set the bags at the curb. I used to do the same thing. After learning what fallen leaves do, I now leave many of them where they land.

Leaves are not trash. They are food and shelter. As they break down, they return nutrients to the soil, which feeds the grass and trees the following spring. A thin layer of leaves over a garden bed also holds moisture in the ground and protects plant roots from hard frost.

Fallen leaves are also a winter home for many small creatures. Some caterpillars spend the cold months wrapped in leaves before becoming moths and butterflies. Beetles, spiders, and earthworms shelter there too, and songbirds depend on these creatures for food in early spring. When we bag the leaves, we bag up part of the food chain.

My neighbor, Mr. Okafor, disagrees with me. He points out that a thick pile of wet leaves can smother a lawn and leave brown patches. He is right about that. A heavy layer left on grass all winter can do damage. That is why I do not leave leaves everywhere. I mow over the leaves on my lawn so they break into small pieces, and I rake the rest into garden beds and under shrubs.

A yard does not have to choose between neat and natural. With a little planning, it can be both.$q$, 'draft')
