-- Passage rg7-authors-purpose-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('149f235a-7156-50bf-a282-bd3f18a3be01', 'rg7-authors-purpose-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Let Students Paint the Underpass$q$, $q$The concrete walls of the Lindell Street underpass are gray, cracked, and often covered in scribbled tags. The city plans to repaint them gray again this summer, as it has done every few years. I propose a different plan: invite students from the three nearby schools to design and paint a mural there.

A mural would turn a dreary walkway into a place people enjoy. More importantly, it would give young artists a real project with a real audience. Students would learn to plan a large design, work as a team, and finish a job on a deadline. Those skills matter in any career, not only in art.

The city's public works director has raised two concerns. First, she worries that a mural will cost more than gray paint. Second, she fears that a mural will be covered by new tags within months. Both concerns deserve an answer.

On cost, the art teachers at all three schools have offered to supervise without pay, and the hardware store on Lindell Street has offered to donate brushes. The paint would cost about twice as much as plain gray, but the mural would not need repainting every few years.

On tagging, the evidence points the other way. In towns that have tried student murals, people tend to leave painted walls alone. A plain gray wall looks like an empty page. A mural that a community made together looks like something worth protecting.

The city council will vote on the repainting plan in May. I hope its members will choose color.$q$, 'draft')
