-- Passage rg7-authors-purpose-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0c47699a-603d-5c95-935b-8305ebd84dc1', 'rg7-authors-purpose-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Fix It First$q$, $q$When a toaster stops working or a jacket loses a button, many people simply buy a new one. Prices for small items are often low, and repairs can seem like more trouble than they are worth. I understand that thinking, but I believe we should try to fix things before we throw them away.

Once a month, the community center on Birch Lane holds a repair night. Volunteers who know about sewing, electronics, and bicycles sit at long tables. Neighbors bring in broken lamps, torn backpacks, and wobbly chairs. Last month, the volunteers fixed forty-one of the fifty items people brought. Most repairs took less than half an hour, and none of them cost anything.

Fixing things saves more than money. Every repaired item is one less item in a landfill. Repair also teaches useful skills. At repair night, owners sit beside the volunteers and learn how to do the job themselves next time. Twelve-year-old Priya Nandakumar learned to replace a bike chain in October. In November, she was showing other kids how to do it.

Some people argue that new products are safer and work better than old ones. In some cases, that is true. A heater with a cracked cord, for example, should be replaced, not taped. The volunteers know this and will tell owners when an item cannot be safely repaired. But a lamp with a loose switch or a chair with a wobbly leg is not dangerous. It just needs a little attention.

Before you throw something out, bring it to Birch Lane. You may leave with your item fixed and a new skill besides.$q$, 'draft')
